Thorsten Jens wrote:
> Hi,
> 
> I've recently stumbled into DEC/VMS administration, so I'm not really
> an expert yet.
> 
> When connecting (serial interface) to one of HSZ50 controllers, I got a
> couple of these messages:
> 
> 
> --15-NOV-2005 02:09:05-- Instance Code: 0328450A

    Instance code 0328450A translates to, "The disk device reported
standard SCSI Sense Data".  This is the usual message reported for
a soft error (see below).

>   Template: 81.(51)
>   Occurred on 15-NOV-2005 at 02:09:04
>   Power On Time: 7. Years, 70. Days, 4. Hours, 29. Minutes, 17. Seconds
>   Controller Model: HSZ50-AX
>   Serial Number: ZG81641143 Hardware Version:  A02(02)
>   Firmware Version: V52Z(52)

>   Unit Number: 203.(00CB)

    The disk is unit 203 (DKA203? I don't recall what device
type HSZ's give to their disk devices).

>   Unit Software Version: 1.(01) Unit Hardware Version: 52.(34)
>   Retry Level: 1. Retries: 0.
>   Port: 6. Target: 1. LUN: 0.

    The physical disk is at PTL 6 1 0.  If you do a SHOW DISK
on the controller, you'll see it listed.

>   SCSI Device Type: 0.(00)
>   Device ID: "RZ1CB-CS (C) DEC" Device Serial Number: "640R8GC3"
>   Device Firmware Revision Level: "0844"
>   SCSI Command Opcode: 40.(28)
>   Sense Data Qualifiers: 128.(80)
>   SCSI Sense Data:
>    Error Code: 112.(70) {current command execution}
>    Information field is valid
>    Segment: 0.(00)
>    Sense Key: 1.(01) RECOVERED ERROR

    A "soft" error.  If this happens only very occassionally,
there may be no problem.  Unfortunately, on HSG80's with ACS
firmware prior to V8.8-F2, the bad block replacement won't
occur and you keep getting the same disk blocks flagging
errors (sigh).  I don't have any first hand experience with
HSZ's, just a warnging.

>    ILI: 0 EOM: 0 FM: 0
>    Information: 99930100
>    Additional Sense Length: 10.(0A)
>    Command-Specific Information: 00000000
>    ASC: 23.(17) ASCQ: 3.(03)
>    FRU: 223.(DF) Sense-Key Specific: 110080
>   Instance Code: 0328450A
> 
> 
> I figure maybe a drive is slowly starting to rot, but I'm not sure. Is
> this something to worry about?

    We track these.  If they occur less than once every few months,
not to worry.  If you start logging a bunch of them over a few days
or a few weeks, consider replacing the drive.

> Bonus question: How do HSZ50s/HSZ80s react to having the serial cable
> pulled (and replaced)? Do they stop like VAXen or don't they mind?

    No problem pulling the cable.

	-Ken
